            {
              "id": "CVE-2025-5278",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "coreutils: Heap Buffer Under-Read in GNU Coreutils sort via Key Specification"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A flaw was found in GNU Coreutils. The sort utility's begfield() function is vulnerable to a heap buffer under-read. The program may access memory outside the allocated buffer if a user runs a crafted command using the traditional key format. A malicious input could lead to a crash or leak sensitive data."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-5278",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2025-5278\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: coreutils\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2025-5278](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-5278)\nA flaw was found in GNU Coreutils. The sort utility's begfield() function is vulnerable to a heap buffer under-read. The program may access memory outside the allocated buffer if a user runs a crafted command using the traditional key format. A malicious input could lead to a crash or leak sensitive data.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2025-5278**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|coreutils||[CVE-2025-5278](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-5278)|\n\nA flaw was found in GNU Coreutils. The sort utility's begfield() function is vulnerable to a heap buffer under-read. The program may access memory outside the allocated buffer if a user runs a crafted command using the traditional key format. A malicious input could lead to a crash or leak sensitive data."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-56391",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "coreutils: GNU coreutils uniq: Denial of Service and information disclosure via out-of-bounds read with multibyte input"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "GNU coreutils uniq is vulnerable to an out\u2011of\u2011bounds read due to incorrect handling of multibyte input when the -w (--check-chars) option is used. The find_field() function miscalculates the byte length of characters by repeatedly processing a fixed pointer instead of advancing through the input, resulting in an inflated length value. \nThis incorrect length is later used in a memcmp operation, causing reads beyond the allocated buffer when processing crafted multibyte input.\n\nWhen running GNU coreutils uniq with attacker-provided arguments, this behavior leads to a crash and potential adjacent heap memory exposure.\n\n\nThis issue has been fixed in the commit d64e35a8a4c0e4608321433e0d84d917e4e36371."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-56391",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-56391\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: coreutils\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-56391](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-56391)\nGNU coreutils uniq is vulnerable to an out\u2011of\u2011bounds read due to incorrect handling of multibyte input when the -w (--check-chars) option is used. The find_field() function miscalculates the byte length of characters by repeatedly processing a fixed pointer instead of advancing through the input, resulting in an inflated length value. \nThis incorrect length is later used in a memcmp operation, causing reads beyond the allocated buffer when processing crafted multibyte input.\n\nWhen running GNU coreutils uniq with attacker-provided arguments, this behavior leads to a crash and potential adjacent heap memory exposure.\n\n\nThis issue has been fixed in the commit d64e35a8a4c0e4608321433e0d84d917e4e36371.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-56391**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|coreutils||[CVE-2026-56391](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-56391)|\n\nGNU coreutils uniq is vulnerable to an out\u2011of\u2011bounds read due to incorrect handling of multibyte input when the -w (--check-chars) option is used. The find_field() function miscalculates the byte length of characters by repeatedly processing a fixed pointer instead of advancing through the input, resulting in an inflated length value. \nThis incorrect length is later used in a memcmp operation, causing reads beyond the allocated buffer when processing crafted multibyte input.\n\nWhen running GNU coreutils uniq with attacker-provided arguments, this behavior leads to a crash and potential adjacent heap memory exposure.\n\n\nThis issue has been fixed in the commit d64e35a8a4c0e4608321433e0d84d917e4e36371."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-56392",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "coreutils: GNU coreutils unexpand: Denial of Service via crafted tab stop values"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "GNU coreutils unexpand is vulnerable to a heap-based buffer overflow due to an integer overflow during buffer allocation when processing large tab stop (-t) values. The multiplication used to calculate the allocation size can wrap around, resulting in an undersized buffer.\nWhen processing crafted input, subsequent writes exceed the allocated memory, leading to an out\u2011of\u2011bounds heap write.\n\nWhen running GNU coreutils unexpand with attacker-provided large tab stop (-t) arguments, this behavior leads to a crash and potentially achieve a heap write primitive depending on memory layout.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThis issue has been fixed in the commit b60a159fdc5bfcf9988d3a4cb6f53abe8ad5d35d"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-56392",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-56392\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: coreutils\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-56392](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-56392)\nGNU coreutils unexpand is vulnerable to a heap-based buffer overflow due to an integer overflow during buffer allocation when processing large tab stop (-t) values. The multiplication used to calculate the allocation size can wrap around, resulting in an undersized buffer.\nWhen processing crafted input, subsequent writes exceed the allocated memory, leading to an out\u2011of\u2011bounds heap write.\n\nWhen running GNU coreutils unexpand with attacker-provided large tab stop (-t) arguments, this behavior leads to a crash and potentially achieve a heap write primitive depending on memory layout.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThis issue has been fixed in the commit b60a159fdc5bfcf9988d3a4cb6f53abe8ad5d35d",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-56392**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|coreutils||[CVE-2026-56392](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-56392)|\n\nGNU coreutils unexpand is vulnerable to a heap-based buffer overflow due to an integer overflow during buffer allocation when processing large tab stop (-t) values. The multiplication used to calculate the allocation size can wrap around, resulting in an undersized buffer.\nWhen processing crafted input, subsequent writes exceed the allocated memory, leading to an out\u2011of\u2011bounds heap write.\n\nWhen running GNU coreutils unexpand with attacker-provided large tab stop (-t) arguments, this behavior leads to a crash and potentially achieve a heap write primitive depending on memory layout.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThis issue has been fixed in the commit b60a159fdc5bfcf9988d3a4cb6f53abe8ad5d35d"
              },
